WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.559
Only fifteen years ago, few Parisians dared to cross the city by bike. Cars drove fast, cycle lanes were rare and many people found it dangerous. Today the landscape has changed: at rush hour, on some major roads, you now see more bikes than cars.

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:39.444
Several reasons explain this success. The city has built hundreds of kilometres of new lanes, especially since 2020. Electric bikes also let older or less sporty people make long journeys without arriving at the office exhausted. And for many residents, cycling is simply faster than the metro over medium distances.

00:00:39.694 --> 00:00:57.327
Not everyone is convinced, though. Some shopkeepers complain that their customers can no longer park in front of their shop. Pedestrians say they are afraid of cyclists who ride on the pavement or don't stop at red lights. As for taxi drivers, they spend more time in traffic jams.

00:00:57.577 --> 00:01:09.138
For the city hall, the choice is clear: fewer cars mean less noise and cleaner air. But for everyone to live alongside each other, each person will have to learn to share the street.
